# General Coding Principles

-   Think minimalistic; always prefer simple solutions.
-   Avoid code duplication when possible; check other areas of the codebase that might have similar code and functionality.
-   Careful to only make changes that are requested or are confident they're well understood and related to the change being requested.
-   Do not introduce new patterns when fixing issues or bugs without exhausting all options for existing implementation; if done, be sure to remove the old implementation to prevent duplicate logic.
-   Keep code clean and organized.
-   Use comments to explain complex logic or non-obvious code sections.
-   Use error logs often to troubleshoot functionality.
-   Avoid writing one-off scripts in files if possible, especially if the script is likely to only be run once; place them in a dedicated 'scripts' folder instead.
-   Never overwrite any .env file without confirmation.
-   Break things up into multiple, single-purpose functions.
-   Prioritize modularity and the Single Responsibility Principle.
-   The names of variables and functions should accurately describe their purpose and behavior.
-   Rate limit API points.
-   Enable CAPTCHA.
-   Use Git commits to keep checkpoints.

## Frontend Specific Rules (Flutter/Dart)
-   Use Material Design.
-   Create reusable widgets for specific UI elements or functionalities.
-   Categorize and structure Flutter project folders logically (e.g., features, services, models, providers).
-   A Dart file should not contain more than 300 lines.
-   Use named routes for navigation instead of direct route-based navigation.

## packages and frameworks
-   Document when a new package is introduced and why.  Explain the context of its usage. 